his willingness to jump in

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:42.760
and to be able to share
so many important tips

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:43.910
with all of us.

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:47.190
So by way of introduction,
Dr. Obo Addy is currently

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:50.590
a sleep physician at the
University of Michigan Health West.

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:53.800
He also has an appointment as
a clinical associate professor

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:56.200
in the departments of
medicine and psychiatry

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:58.080
at Michigan State University.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:00:59.930
He's had previous positions at places

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:02.180
like Case Western Reserve University,

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:05.340
also at Emory University
School of Medicine in Atlanta.

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:07.820
And he's someone who
has been deeply involved

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:11.440
in both psychiatry and sleep
medicine for over 30 years,

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:14.240
so has expertise on both sides
of the house, so to speak,

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.470
and the intersection between the two.

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:19.150
He's a husband, he's also
a father of four children

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:21.510
and a grandfather of five.

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:24.240
And he's someone who continues
to be invested in many

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:26.440
different ways in the
community, through his church,

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:28.310
but has also done things
in the broader community,

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:30.940
even in his semi-retired state right now,

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:33.070
he's been involved in
setting up sleep clinics

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:34.517
in Ghana for the past several years,

00:01:34.517 --> 00:01:37.530
and so has continued to
make sure that the expertise

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:40.280
that he has and what
he's been able to bring

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:42.790
to the community and to
different fields of medicine

00:01:42.790 --> 00:01:45.310
continues to have impact in lives.

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:48.310
So I'm grateful to be
able to welcome him today.

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:50.610
Just as a little bit of a full circle,

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:54.130
I remember growing up and
watching him on a CNN interview

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:56.540
where he was talking about
his work and being very proud

00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:57.750
and awe-inspired.

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:00.400
So it's full circle for
me to be able to host him

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:03.290
on my own podcast and grateful
to welcome Dr. Obo Addy

00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:05.168
to the podcast today.

00:02:05.168 --> 00:02:06.823
- Thank you, thank you very much.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:08.850
- Thanks for being here.

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:12.150
And I'm just excited to be
able to move this conversation

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:12.983
in a lot of ways.

00:02:12.983 --> 00:02:14.050
And this has been something that's been

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:15.390
in the back of my mind for a while.

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:17.990
Just the benefits that I've
had growing up with you

00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:19.550
and all the insights that
you've shared with us

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:20.383
over the years.

00:02:20.383 --> 00:02:21.216
So

00:02:30.079 --> 00:02:32.246
(laughs)

00:02:35.531 --> 00:02:36.364
That's true.

00:02:36.364 --> 00:02:37.880
Maybe that's what peaked
my interest initially,

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:40.220
even though I think at the
time I was trying to evolve

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:42.290
in my mind and go far
away from your field,

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:45.423
which I was very unsuccessful at. (laughs)

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:49.520
At some point we have to find
that picture if you have it.

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:53.660
Excellent.

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:55.570
Well, as my listeners know,

00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:56.550
I always like to just check in

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:59.430
and see how my guests are doing overall,

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:01.140
just with all the different
things that you're doing

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:04.350
in your semi-retirement with
your family responsibilities.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:06.170
Even the ways that you've
helped my family out

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:08.100
in the last few weeks and the sleep clinic

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:09.520
that you're doing in Ghana,

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:12.010
how is everything in your own life,

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:15.090
especially in the midst
of the ongoing pandemic?

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:17.110
- Well, COVID affects everything.

00:03:17.110 --> 00:03:21.400
It did affect the planned trips to Ghana,

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:22.900
but on a personal level,

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:25.470
one of the things I found
a little disruptive was

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:29.360
the most recent trip to
Ghana, actually last two,

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:32.500
one in April and another in July.

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:34.730
What you have to go
through with COVID testing

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:37.100
that within a two, three week period,

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:39.950
I had to have three COVID tests,

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:40.970
which are not pleasant.

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:45.230
So then there's one test
72 hours before the flight.

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:46.690
And then when you get to Ghana,

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:50.040
you have a rapid test at the
airport and you stay there

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.821
for the result before you
can leave the airport.

00:03:52.821 --> 00:03:53.900
And then when you are coming back,

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:55.360
you have to have another COVID test.

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:58.720
So for me, having three COVID tests

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:01.920
in a two, three week period
was not very pleasant.

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:03.823
But to put it in perspective, I mean,

00:04:03.823 --> 00:04:06.030
I had the understanding,
the reason for this

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:08.730
it's for my own health and
for the health of others.

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:10.830
And I think a lot of people
are going through this

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:14.510
where there is some
discomfort or annoyance

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:16.810
about some of the things
that we have to go through,

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:20.020
but I think we have to
be open to accepting

00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:22.510
these inconveniences for the overall good.

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:26.520
So that's my personal
experience with COVID

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:27.700
and how to deal with.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:28.717
- No, that makes a lot of sense.

00:04:28.717 --> 00:04:32.990
And it sounds like these
weren't the painless tests,

00:04:32.990 --> 00:04:34.840
it sounds like you'd had the deeper swabs

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:36.490
and all that, as well, and.

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:39.700
- Yes, and the very first
one that I had, I said,

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:42.030
it's like somebody is
trying to poke a needle

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:43.190
into the base of your brain.

00:04:43.190 --> 00:04:45.093
- Oh boy. (laughs)

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:49.150
Wow, that is saying something
for you, as a psychiatrist,

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:52.562
to use that analogy, as well.

00:04:52.562 --> 00:04:53.700
But I think you said it well, too,

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:56.070
in terms of just people
being willing to have

00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:57.750
the inconveniences for their own health

00:04:57.750 --> 00:04:59.850
and for those around them, as well.

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:01.547
How were things, not
to jump too far ahead,

00:05:01.547 --> 00:05:03.530
but how were things in Ghana
when you went, as well,

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:06.610
in terms of people's
perspectives about COVID

00:05:06.610 --> 00:05:08.860
and a willingness to kind of abide

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:11.930
by some of these practices
and things like that?

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:14.400
- People have been fairly accepting.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:18.426
I mean, there are no overt protests.

00:05:18.426 --> 00:05:19.670
There are people who are comfortable,

00:05:19.670 --> 00:05:22.000
but as opposed to maybe things we see

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:23.760
in the Western part of the world,

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:25.970
people are more accepting
of what their government's

00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:27.450
position on things is.

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:31.240
I think it's because of
maybe the traditional way

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:34.690
of things and the way things are set up,

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:37.670
that people look up more to
authority in a lot of ways

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:38.530
or to the elders.

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:43.020
So the president, in that
sense, is the most senior elder

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:43.853
for the country.

00:05:43.853 --> 00:05:45.790
So if he said, this is good for us,

00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:47.860
although people may not like it,

00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:48.990
they are generally accepting

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:50.760
and people are wearing their masks.

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:52.870
I mean, there are people who
are not following the rules,

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:56.680
but by-and-large, people are
accepting and recognizing

00:05:56.680 --> 00:05:59.080
that there is a problem going on,

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:01.793
it's a pandemic and people
want to see it control.

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:03.957
- Oh, that's encouraging to hear.

00:06:03.957 --> 00:06:07.396
And it seems like with that
role with the elders, too,

00:06:07.396 --> 00:06:09.630
there's two sides to that
because there's the earned,

00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:10.700
there's the respect that's there,

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:13.480
but then it seems like it
also has to be kept, as well,

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:17.010
and not used in the way that
actually is harmful to people.

00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:20.180
So there's a lot of weight
there, as well, in some ways.

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:21.570
- And maybe that's what has led to some

00:06:21.570 --> 00:06:24.890
of the dictatorships in
the African continent,

00:06:24.890 --> 00:06:26.610
that because of their respectful elders.

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:29.550
Yeah, some of these leaders
have abused that privilege

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:32.553
and we've seen dictators
there, unfortunately.

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:35.367
- Yeah, no, that makes a lot of sense.

00:06:35.367 --> 00:06:38.060
And it's encouraging to hear
that things are in that,

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:41.270
moving in that way now, at least
in Ghana, in a lot of ways,

00:06:41.270 --> 00:06:43.390
It actually leads to the next question.

00:06:43.390 --> 00:06:46.490
Just thinking about your
profession as someone

00:06:46.490 --> 00:06:47.500
in sleep medicine, as well,

00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:51.140
because I know obviously
personally from your trajectory

00:06:51.140 --> 00:06:52.880
and your path going into psychiatry,

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:55.350
there were aspects that came up initially

00:06:55.350 --> 00:06:59.820
in terms of whether that was
favored by the government,

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:01.720
the types of work you're doing
and how it was looked upon

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:03.980
and how that shaped your own path.

00:07:03.980 --> 00:07:05.680
So I think it would be
helpful for our listeners,

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:08.170
if you could share how
you got into psychiatry

00:07:08.170 --> 00:07:09.460
and sleep medicine in the first place.

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:12.070
And I guess the intersection
between your personal story

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:14.070
and the role of the government,

00:07:14.070 --> 00:07:16.000
as much as you're willing to share.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:16.833
- Yeah.

00:07:16.833 --> 00:07:20.520
I think God gives us different
interests and affinities

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:22.210
for certain things.

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:27.210
So I don't know that I can
pinpoint what led to my interest

00:07:27.890 --> 00:07:30.480
in psychiatry, but there
was a very early interest

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:33.150
in the things of the
mind and how that works.

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:36.640
And I think that also
relates to my Christian faith

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:39.050
and background as to what God is doing

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:42.540
and it's not just all the things
you can measure that much.

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:46.010
So there was an initial
interest in psychiatry,

00:07:46.010 --> 00:07:48.780
but in medical school in
Ghana, that was downplayed.

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:52.630
The idea was we have big
problems with infectious diseases

00:07:52.630 --> 00:07:53.463
and other things.

00:07:53.463 --> 00:07:56.620
And why would anyone want
to focus on psychiatry?

00:07:56.620 --> 00:07:58.630
That is not that important.

00:07:58.630 --> 00:08:03.170
So for me, the whole
thing came more to light

00:08:03.170 --> 00:08:05.793
when I was doing my
rural service in Ghana.

00:08:06.890 --> 00:08:09.190
And as a general medical practitioner,

00:08:09.190 --> 00:08:12.390
you are doing everything
that one year or two years

00:08:12.390 --> 00:08:13.930
out of medical school.

00:08:13.930 --> 00:08:16.630
Doing everything means I was doing things

00:08:16.630 --> 00:08:18.860
like hernia surgeries.

00:08:18.860 --> 00:08:20.510
And one of the most satisfying was doing

00:08:20.510 --> 00:08:23.130
emergency caesarian sections.

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:25.510
And to have a healthy mother

00:08:25.510 --> 00:08:28.350
and a healthy baby afterwards.

00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:30.593
But then what struck me
as I was running a clinic

00:08:30.593 --> 00:08:33.320
that I was at there were
people who were coming in,

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:38.320
especially some of the women
from maybe abusive families

00:08:38.540 --> 00:08:42.980
who repeatedly will be at
a clinic seeking some care,

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:47.980
and there was no physical
care or need at that point.

00:08:48.630 --> 00:08:52.060
It quickly became obvious
to me that these people had

00:08:52.060 --> 00:08:54.900
emotional problems that
nobody was dealing with.

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:56.790
And they were coming to
the clinic and coming

00:08:56.790 --> 00:08:58.203
to the doctor for help.

00:08:59.350 --> 00:09:03.260
But then, the thing that maybe
promoted more the interest

00:09:03.260 --> 00:09:06.120
to pursue my interest
in psychiatry was this.

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:09.470
I knew how to handle
most medical emergencies.

00:09:09.470 --> 00:09:12.540
I knew how to treat routine
things like the malaria.

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:15.605
Like I said, I could handle
emergency (indistinct).

00:09:15.605 --> 00:09:17.840
I could handle emergency
caesarian sections.

00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:22.680
I had no idea how to handle
any psychiatric emergencies.

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:25.840
So it strike me that there
was this clear need there.

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:28.180
And although this had been downplayed,

00:09:28.180 --> 00:09:30.810
what my professors were saying
was not really accurate,

00:09:30.810 --> 00:09:32.600
that there was no need,
there was a huge need.

00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:37.160
So then that led me to follow
the interest and the affinity

00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:40.860
that I say God had given
me to go into a field

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:43.570
that will help those who
are emotionally distressed

00:09:43.570 --> 00:09:45.210
and needed help.

00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:46.690
- Oh, that's encouraging to hear.

00:09:46.690 --> 00:09:48.760
And so how did you take those steps

00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:51.830
if the avenue wasn't clear in a sense,

00:09:51.830 --> 00:09:54.030
if the precedent wasn't there also?

00:09:54.030 --> 00:09:56.740
- Yeah. So then I did talk to,

00:09:56.740 --> 00:09:59.340
well, he was actually an uncle by marriage

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:01.750
who was a psychiatrist who I looked up to.

00:10:01.750 --> 00:10:04.230
So I asked him how one would do this.

00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:07.220
So he did encourage me, but then he said,

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:11.210
well, you may have a glorified
idea of what psychiatry is.

00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:13.850
So why don't you get a clearer idea?

00:10:13.850 --> 00:10:18.300
So he invited me to work
with him for some time

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:22.810
attached to the main psychiatric
hospital in the capital,

00:10:22.810 --> 00:10:24.060
in Accra.

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:27.723
And this was the old type asylum.

00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:31.510
I mean, there were good rooms,

00:10:31.510 --> 00:10:36.130
but then some of them were
basically being put there

00:10:36.130 --> 00:10:37.310
just for custodial care.

00:10:37.310 --> 00:10:40.220
And there wasn't much good treatment.

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:42.690
In a sense, he opened my eyes to see

00:10:42.690 --> 00:10:44.890
maybe the worst part of it.

00:10:44.890 --> 00:10:46.730
That is that, what he said,
if you want to go into this,

00:10:46.730 --> 00:10:48.900
you want to see the true picture.

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:51.750
But that heightened my interest
rather than dumping it.

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:52.820
And I felt like, well,

00:10:52.820 --> 00:10:54.970
this is something that
I was willing to do.

00:10:55.870 --> 00:10:57.820
So he did encourage me along the way,

00:10:57.820 --> 00:11:02.010
but there was no avenue
for going to psychiatry

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:05.303
and to make things a little
bit more interesting,

00:11:06.490 --> 00:11:10.030
the head of surgery had
developed an interest in me.

00:11:10.030 --> 00:11:11.640
And I suppose I was good with my hands

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:12.860
with surgical interest.

00:11:12.860 --> 00:11:15.390
So you might admit to go into surgery

00:11:15.390 --> 00:11:18.830
and I was going to have
full sponsorship to do that

00:11:18.830 --> 00:11:21.880
and there was no avenue
for going to psychiatry.

00:11:21.880 --> 00:11:25.770
And yet I felt like this is
what God was calling me to do.

00:11:25.770 --> 00:11:29.550
So when the chief of surgery asked me,

00:11:29.550 --> 00:11:32.170
invited me to come in to surgery,

00:11:32.170 --> 00:11:33.890
I was forced to make a decision then.

00:11:33.890 --> 00:11:35.740
I did write to him to say, no,

00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:36.880
I'm not going to surgery.

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:38.930
I want to go into psychiatry.

00:11:38.930 --> 00:11:40.270
And he reacted, like,

00:11:40.270 --> 00:11:44.170
why would anyone choose
to go into psychiatry?

00:11:44.170 --> 00:11:46.830
Especially since there was no clear path

00:11:46.830 --> 00:11:48.475
for me to get there.

00:11:48.475 --> 00:11:52.070
And there was a clear, easy
path for me to become a surgeon.

00:11:52.070 --> 00:11:53.940
But yeah, I did talk to my uncle

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:56.180
and then it's a whole, long story.

00:11:56.180 --> 00:11:57.910
But then the steps fell into place

00:11:57.910 --> 00:12:02.520
and I did end up going into
psychiatric training at Duke,

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:05.052
where the young man who is
interviewing me went to school.

00:12:05.052 --> 00:12:07.127
(laughs)

00:12:07.127 --> 00:12:07.960
(speakers talking over each other)

00:12:07.960 --> 00:12:09.050
That's another whole, long story.

00:12:09.050 --> 00:12:11.080
- Full circle in many ways, so.

00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:11.913
It's always, I mean,

00:12:11.913 --> 00:12:15.150
I always feel like I learn more
each time I hear this story.

00:12:15.150 --> 00:12:17.910
So it's also encouraging
to hear your willingness

00:12:17.910 --> 00:12:19.930
to follow the passion
even though others weren't

00:12:19.930 --> 00:12:22.900
necessarily supportive in that sense.

00:12:22.900 --> 00:12:25.360
And then how did the sleep
part become integrated

00:12:25.360 --> 00:12:27.000
into this story?

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:30.490
- Yes, in a sense, as I
was thinking about this,

00:12:30.490 --> 00:12:32.320
it's the same kind of a story

00:12:33.190 --> 00:12:37.160
in terms of the human interest,
the need to help others.

00:12:37.160 --> 00:12:39.370
Like I said, I was in
(indistinct) medicine

00:12:39.370 --> 00:12:42.490
and these ladies were
coming in and they had

00:12:42.490 --> 00:12:45.510
psychiatric needs that
nobody was dealing with,

00:12:45.510 --> 00:12:48.580
that nobody knew what to do about it.

00:12:48.580 --> 00:12:52.080
It was in my first year
of training at Duke

00:12:52.960 --> 00:12:57.960
that a patient came in and I
labeled him, the lazy farmer.

00:13:00.490 --> 00:13:02.710
So this guy belonged to a farming family

00:13:02.710 --> 00:13:06.180
and he was always sleepy and tired

00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:07.580
and he would get out of the tractor

00:13:07.580 --> 00:13:09.060
and his brothers would see him

00:13:09.060 --> 00:13:12.230
out in the middle of the
field, sleeping on the tractor

00:13:12.230 --> 00:13:13.420
and they said he was lazy.

00:13:13.420 --> 00:13:15.870
I mean, he didn't like to
work like his brothers.

00:13:16.790 --> 00:13:19.700
Then somebody said, well, maybe
he has some medical problem.

00:13:19.700 --> 00:13:22.470
Maybe it was a seizure
problem or some other thing,

00:13:22.470 --> 00:13:26.300
so he was brought to
Duke for a full workup.

00:13:26.300 --> 00:13:31.300
And this was in the workup
that my professor then,

00:13:31.390 --> 00:13:34.510
the professor who was training me in EEG.

00:13:34.510 --> 00:13:35.870
I was in the psychiatric program,

00:13:35.870 --> 00:13:38.310
but I was doing the EEG training,

00:13:38.310 --> 00:13:40.250
which was combined with it.

00:13:40.250 --> 00:13:44.070
And he said, maybe this
guy has sleep apnea,

00:13:44.070 --> 00:13:46.060
maybe he's not lazy after all.

00:13:46.060 --> 00:13:49.960
And that was the first I heard
of anything like sleep apnea.

00:13:49.960 --> 00:13:53.140
To not make it a long
story, he did have a workup.

00:13:53.140 --> 00:13:56.360
There were not established
sleep clinics then,

00:13:56.360 --> 00:13:58.920
but he had workup, so that's
when he had treatment,

00:13:58.920 --> 00:14:01.210
but that thing stuck in my mind.

00:14:01.210 --> 00:14:04.770
This guy had a genuine
medical sleep problem.

00:14:04.770 --> 00:14:07.770
It was labeled by family
and friends as lazy.

00:14:07.770 --> 00:14:11.890
So the thought was how many
other so-called lazy farmers are

00:14:11.890 --> 00:14:16.500
there who need help, who
nobody's giving the help to?

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:17.430
So that's what I'm saying,

00:14:17.430 --> 00:14:20.070
maybe in the sense as
I was looking at this,

00:14:20.070 --> 00:14:21.810
we have our own way of processing things.

00:14:21.810 --> 00:14:26.290
So there's the rural people,
psychiatric patients in Ghana,

00:14:26.290 --> 00:14:27.950
nobody was taking care of.

00:14:27.950 --> 00:14:30.200
How many are there that needed help?

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:31.650
Then there's the lazy farmer.

00:14:33.491 --> 00:14:36.090
How many more lazy farmers
are there who need help?

00:14:36.090 --> 00:14:39.530
So again, the interest was
going to this thing, if you can,

00:14:39.530 --> 00:14:41.960
to try and help people with that.

00:14:41.960 --> 00:14:45.610
But it didn't mean that I
went directly into that.

00:14:45.610 --> 00:14:47.580
I mean, I had an interest there,

00:14:47.580 --> 00:14:49.610
but there was no avenue to follow it.

00:14:49.610 --> 00:14:52.720
Again, this looks like
the same story before.

00:14:52.720 --> 00:14:56.140
It was years after I had
left Duke that I was working

00:14:56.140 --> 00:14:59.410
elsewhere where an old
professor from Duke came

00:14:59.410 --> 00:15:00.560
to give grand rounds.

00:15:00.560 --> 00:15:04.120
And he says, we want to
develop a sleep program.

00:15:04.120 --> 00:15:06.150
I mean, he was at everything.

00:15:06.150 --> 00:15:08.160
And I know your background
that you were combining

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:11.370
psychiatry and EEG things.

00:15:11.370 --> 00:15:14.210
And I think you'd be an
ideal person to help us

00:15:14.210 --> 00:15:15.960
get a program going.

00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:17.210
So it was an invitation.

00:15:17.210 --> 00:15:18.390
And then,

00:15:18.390 --> 00:15:20.640
so I didn't realize that
this interest was known

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:24.760
to any others, but obviously
I've become known to others.

00:15:24.760 --> 00:15:29.470
So then I did pick up where
I had left off, so to speak,

00:15:29.470 --> 00:15:31.320
and that's how I came into sleep.

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:33.620
It's a long way to say
it, but I hope that.

00:15:33.620 --> 00:15:34.910
- No, that's good and then it seems

00:15:34.910 --> 00:15:36.860
like some of the passion was there,

00:15:36.860 --> 00:15:40.060
but you didn't necessarily
have the opportunity initially

00:15:40.060 --> 00:15:43.320
and then it still came
from an invitation later on

00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:45.420
that you were able to take advantage of,

00:15:45.420 --> 00:15:46.620
which seems really important.

00:15:46.620 --> 00:15:49.150
It's also interesting
hearing the story because it,

00:15:49.150 --> 00:15:51.060
in the sleep side, seems to parallel

00:15:51.060 --> 00:15:53.700
a lot of what's happened with
mental health in this country

00:15:53.700 --> 00:15:56.390
and globally, as people
have had better awareness

00:15:56.390 --> 00:15:59.940
and try not to label
people as just, you know,

00:15:59.940 --> 00:16:02.280
the aunt or uncle who's a
little bit off or this and that.

00:16:02.280 --> 00:16:03.330
But with the same thing with sleep,

00:16:03.330 --> 00:16:05.270
because the farmer was
just being labeled as lazy

00:16:05.270 --> 00:16:08.170
without a true awareness of.

00:16:08.170 --> 00:16:10.200
Do you think that's moved over the years

00:16:10.200 --> 00:16:11.040
or are people still,

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:12.560
are there still things
people are unaware of

00:16:12.560 --> 00:16:16.210
in terms of sleep and how
sleep problems can affect

00:16:16.210 --> 00:16:17.043
our daily lives?

00:16:17.043 --> 00:16:18.680
- I think it's improved a lot.

00:16:18.680 --> 00:16:22.574
I mean a lot from, yeah,
even when I started out was

00:16:22.574 --> 00:16:25.220
the early phases, about 30 years ago,

00:16:25.220 --> 00:16:27.660
but when we moved to Grand Rapids,

00:16:27.660 --> 00:16:29.980
the clinic maybe about 20 years ago,

00:16:29.980 --> 00:16:32.070
and one of my colleagues,
there were three of us

00:16:32.070 --> 00:16:35.270
that started by basic
training in psychiatry.

00:16:35.270 --> 00:16:38.950
Then there was a neurologist
and then a pulmonologist.

00:16:38.950 --> 00:16:41.860
And it's the pulmonologist
who had the hardest time

00:16:41.860 --> 00:16:43.070
20 years ago.

00:16:43.070 --> 00:16:45.800
A lot of his colleagues
were making statement.

00:16:45.800 --> 00:16:48.134
I mean, you went to medical
school and you spent time

00:16:48.134 --> 00:16:50.163
specializing in pulmonology,

00:16:50.163 --> 00:16:52.024
and now you want to do sleep medicine?

00:16:52.024 --> 00:16:54.590
It's like, you are giving up
all your medical training.

00:16:54.590 --> 00:16:55.860
What is sleep medicine?

00:16:55.860 --> 00:16:57.390
Why would you want to do that?

00:16:57.390 --> 00:16:59.630
So there's a huge move from that.

00:16:59.630 --> 00:17:01.610
So I think there's greater understanding

00:17:01.610 --> 00:17:03.350
even compared to 20 years ago.

00:17:03.350 --> 00:17:05.660
So it's good to see that.

00:17:05.660 --> 00:17:07.260
- Yeah, that's really important.

00:17:08.229 --> 00:17:09.137
What do you think about the understanding?

00:17:09.137 --> 00:17:11.480
'Cause you talked about from
the medical perspective,

00:17:11.480 --> 00:17:13.330
what about in the general public?

00:17:13.330 --> 00:17:15.450
Do people have more of an awareness

00:17:15.450 --> 00:17:19.230
or is there still this
lazy farmer attitude

00:17:19.230 --> 00:17:21.150
that happens when people are struggling

00:17:21.150 --> 00:17:22.573
with disturbed sleep?

00:17:23.530 --> 00:17:25.270
- Yeah, I think in the general public

00:17:25.270 --> 00:17:27.450
there's a greater awareness of this

00:17:27.450 --> 00:17:29.310
and a greater understanding.

00:17:29.310 --> 00:17:31.080
You still hear it, I mean as recently

00:17:31.080 --> 00:17:33.400
as maybe two or three years ago,

00:17:33.400 --> 00:17:37.240
I've had a parent call a
child with narcolepsy, lazy,

00:17:37.240 --> 00:17:40.830
and it became a conflictual
situation where mother was

00:17:40.830 --> 00:17:42.400
more accepting that there was a need

00:17:42.400 --> 00:17:45.090
and all father would
think of no, he's lazy.

00:17:45.090 --> 00:17:47.270
He's not like his brothers, if he were,

00:17:47.270 --> 00:17:50.090
he would do such and such.

00:17:50.090 --> 00:17:51.630
But again, it's moved,

00:17:51.630 --> 00:17:55.150
so there is general
awareness has improved.

00:17:55.150 --> 00:17:57.030
But it still needs to move forward.

00:17:57.030 --> 00:18:02.000
This past week, I read
something that the NIH is giving

00:18:02.000 --> 00:18:05.040
a three-year grant to the
Academy of Sleep Medicine to work

00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:07.470
on increasing the
awareness of sleep apnea.

00:18:07.470 --> 00:18:09.270
So there is a recognition,

00:18:09.270 --> 00:18:13.430
there's a need to do more
public education on this.

00:18:13.430 --> 00:18:15.210
- Yeah, and it seems
important just in terms

00:18:15.210 --> 00:18:19.990
of every aspect of our lives
is how important sleep is.

00:18:19.990 --> 00:18:21.750
So I'm also curious, just,
you know, for our listeners,

00:18:21.750 --> 00:18:23.720
as well, what are some of the things

00:18:23.720 --> 00:18:26.670
that you usually tell people
when people are trying

00:18:26.670 --> 00:18:27.750
to understand what is,

00:18:27.750 --> 00:18:30.030
'cause I've heard you use the
term sleep hygiene before.

00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:31.800
What does good sleep hygiene look like?

00:18:31.800 --> 00:18:35.720
And also, how do people know
if it's a sleep disturbance

00:18:35.720 --> 00:18:38.030
that they're dealing with
or if it's something else

00:18:38.030 --> 00:18:39.930
and how do they know when to get help?

00:18:41.270 --> 00:18:44.310
- Yeah, sleep hygiene refers
to things that you can do

00:18:44.310 --> 00:18:46.680
to promote your sleep, at first.

00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:49.770
Maybe to backup on,

00:18:49.770 --> 00:18:52.270
what are the important
components of sleep?

00:18:52.270 --> 00:18:55.070
Okay, sometimes people will say,

00:18:55.070 --> 00:18:57.360
what is the quality of my sleep?

00:18:57.360 --> 00:19:00.450
If I sleep well, I have
good quality sleep.

00:19:00.450 --> 00:19:04.300
Then it doesn't matter if it's
three hours or four hours,

00:19:04.300 --> 00:19:07.870
but there are things
that we know about sleep.

00:19:07.870 --> 00:19:10.110
Sleep duration is extremely important,

00:19:10.110 --> 00:19:14.390
so, and we can talk about
this later, as well.

00:19:14.390 --> 00:19:17.660
That sleep duration ignored,
regardless of quality,

00:19:17.660 --> 00:19:19.940
if it's not adequate duration can lead

00:19:19.940 --> 00:19:21.300
to major health problems.

00:19:21.300 --> 00:19:24.997
So one of the things that we
need to know about sleep is

00:19:24.997 --> 00:19:27.230
that we need to get enough of it

00:19:28.600 --> 00:19:31.170
and we can't cheat with it.

00:19:31.170 --> 00:19:33.563
It has consequences if
we do not get enough.

00:19:34.690 --> 00:19:39.370
And then the other component
is sleep continuity.

00:19:39.370 --> 00:19:44.030
It's preferable to get all
of our sleep in one block,

00:19:44.030 --> 00:19:45.780
as much as possible.

00:19:45.780 --> 00:19:47.750
When I've talked to medical groups,

00:19:47.750 --> 00:19:50.710
well, especially when I
was in Ghana the last time.

00:19:50.710 --> 00:19:52.660
And then young physicians will say, well,

00:19:52.660 --> 00:19:54.120
I'm on call some of the time.

00:19:54.120 --> 00:19:57.520
But what if I get two hours of sleep now,

00:19:57.520 --> 00:20:00.090
and then I'm awake for the
next four hours and get another

00:20:00.090 --> 00:20:03.663
two hours, and then I get an
hour and a half someplace?

00:20:04.860 --> 00:20:07.150
Even if you have the same total duration,

00:20:07.150 --> 00:20:08.700
if you get eight hours of sleep,

00:20:08.700 --> 00:20:11.470
but it's broken up into
three or four pieces

00:20:11.470 --> 00:20:15.690
with one or two-hour intervals,
as opposed to seven hours,

00:20:15.690 --> 00:20:16.760
I'll use that as an example,

00:20:16.760 --> 00:20:18.880
seven hours of continuous sleep.

00:20:18.880 --> 00:20:23.540
Seven hours of more continuous
sleep is more refreshing

00:20:23.540 --> 00:20:26.550
and has better health consequences

00:20:26.550 --> 00:20:29.470
than a total of eight
hours of sleep broken up

00:20:29.470 --> 00:20:31.410
into five or six segments.

00:20:31.410 --> 00:20:35.120
So the duration is important
and getting consecutive hours

00:20:35.120 --> 00:20:36.373
of sleep is important.

00:20:37.540 --> 00:20:39.203
Then you ask about sleep hygiene,

00:20:40.202 --> 00:20:43.760
they're suppressed to the
poor habits that can make

00:20:43.760 --> 00:20:48.760
your sleep quality less than adequate.

00:20:49.920 --> 00:20:52.680
So things like reading in bed,

00:20:52.680 --> 00:20:55.900
especially if you are
reading on electronic device,

00:20:55.900 --> 00:20:57.860
it will be a low-light intensity,

00:20:57.860 --> 00:21:01.000
but that kind of thing can
still affect your sleep.

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:03.550
It can delay sleep onset.

00:21:03.550 --> 00:21:08.340
Things like stimulants
or even drinking coffee

00:21:08.340 --> 00:21:11.210
and drinking coffee in the
evening can affect you.

00:21:11.210 --> 00:21:15.360
So those are the things
you want to guard against.

00:21:15.360 --> 00:21:20.210
Keeping a regular sleep-wake
pattern is important.

00:21:20.210 --> 00:21:22.130
And again, if it's irregular,

00:21:22.130 --> 00:21:24.540
it will be more harmful than good.

00:21:24.540 --> 00:21:27.460
But there is a component that
most people don't recognize,

00:21:27.460 --> 00:21:29.360
which I think is important.

00:21:29.360 --> 00:21:31.520
Some people will say go
to bed at the same time

00:21:31.520 --> 00:21:33.370
and get up at the same time.

00:21:33.370 --> 00:21:35.980
But the important thing is
to fix your wake-up time,

00:21:35.980 --> 00:21:40.100
and the bedtime, in a sense,
will take care of itself

00:21:40.100 --> 00:21:41.830
or the sleep onset.

00:21:41.830 --> 00:21:44.320
It's easy to remember this
if you look at it this way,

00:21:44.320 --> 00:21:46.670
you can make yourself wake up,

00:21:46.670 --> 00:21:49.113
but you cannot make yourself fall asleep.

00:21:50.040 --> 00:21:52.130
But what people sometimes do,

00:21:52.130 --> 00:21:54.020
especially if they are
having some difficulty

00:21:54.020 --> 00:21:58.453
with their sleep onset, will
say, if I get into bed at 10,

00:22:00.220 --> 00:22:03.260
10 regularly, then I'll fall asleep by 11

00:22:03.260 --> 00:22:04.650
or something like that.

00:22:04.650 --> 00:22:07.540
But if you have problems falling asleep

00:22:07.540 --> 00:22:10.120
and you get into bed
when you're not sleepy,

00:22:10.120 --> 00:22:12.160
your level of anxiety can increase.

00:22:12.160 --> 00:22:15.070
And the longer you're in
bed, the longer you get,

00:22:15.070 --> 00:22:16.697
the more anxious you get,

00:22:16.697 --> 00:22:19.320
and the more difficult
it becomes to sleep.

00:22:19.320 --> 00:22:23.007
So fix your wake-up time and
try not to sleep too much

00:22:23.007 --> 00:22:23.860
during the day.

00:22:23.860 --> 00:22:25.620
We can talk about that later, too.

00:22:25.620 --> 00:22:27.130
And then when it comes to nighttime,

00:22:27.130 --> 00:22:29.680
your body will take care of that time.

00:22:29.680 --> 00:22:31.830
So avoid stimulants at night,

00:22:31.830 --> 00:22:34.780
maintaining good regular
rhythm for other things,

00:22:34.780 --> 00:22:39.780
even including your meals, good,
regular exercise would help

00:22:39.970 --> 00:22:42.780
and avoiding long periods
of sleep during the day.

00:22:42.780 --> 00:22:45.820
These are some of the
components of sleep hygiene.

00:22:45.820 --> 00:22:47.900
Sleep hygiene itself would not cure

00:22:47.900 --> 00:22:50.320
a significant sleep problem.

00:22:50.320 --> 00:22:52.870
But maintaining those
habits can be helpful.

00:22:52.870 --> 00:22:55.020
- Those are all really, really good tips.

00:22:55.020 --> 00:22:57.510
Do you ever get pushback from people?

00:22:57.510 --> 00:22:58.860
'Cause I can imagine people say, oh,

00:22:58.860 --> 00:23:00.640
but I have this and this and this to do,

00:23:00.640 --> 00:23:03.210
or this job situation or this circumstance

00:23:03.210 --> 00:23:04.120
and that's not feasible.

00:23:04.120 --> 00:23:07.483
So what do I do if I can't get
all of those things in place?

00:23:07.483 --> 00:23:11.360
What if I can't get a
consistent wake-up time or,

00:23:11.360 --> 00:23:14.190
you know, family responsibilities
or infants at night?

00:23:14.190 --> 00:23:15.830
That's a whole another topic.

00:23:15.830 --> 00:23:19.140
But how do you help people
in those situations?

00:23:19.140 --> 00:23:21.610
- Yeah, I think it's one
can use that same approach

00:23:21.610 --> 00:23:24.260
you use for anything else.

00:23:24.260 --> 00:23:26.560
In that area, somebody has diabetes,

00:23:26.560 --> 00:23:28.840
so they have to watch
their diet and things.

00:23:28.840 --> 00:23:31.400
But every so often a circumstance comes up

00:23:31.400 --> 00:23:33.150
where they cannot follow that.

00:23:33.150 --> 00:23:36.620
Yeah, so you can break from
that on a short-term basis,

00:23:36.620 --> 00:23:39.080
but not on a long-term, consistent basis.

00:23:39.080 --> 00:23:40.810
Yeah, so you are going through this.

00:23:40.810 --> 00:23:41.970
Somebody has a baby.

00:23:41.970 --> 00:23:44.530
Baby is waking up at night while there's,

00:23:44.530 --> 00:23:46.480
you need to up to help your baby,

00:23:46.480 --> 00:23:49.250
so there's not a whole
lot you can do about that.

00:23:49.250 --> 00:23:51.320
But as soon as possible,

00:23:51.320 --> 00:23:54.120
you want to get back to the good, ongoing,

00:23:54.120 --> 00:23:55.580
consistent practices.

00:23:55.580 --> 00:23:57.560
These are all things
that you want to be doing

00:23:57.560 --> 00:23:59.743
on a long-term, ongoing basis.

00:23:59.743 --> 00:24:01.793
And it's important to keep those in mind.

00:24:02.770 --> 00:24:04.470
But when it comes to sleep hygiene,

00:24:04.470 --> 00:24:07.660
even people who have
significant difficulties,

00:24:07.660 --> 00:24:10.550
usually as soon as you
begin to mention it,

00:24:10.550 --> 00:24:13.040
a lot of patients who say
no, I've done all of that

00:24:13.040 --> 00:24:14.040
and it doesn't work,

00:24:15.149 --> 00:24:18.420
but almost always, when you delve into it,

00:24:18.420 --> 00:24:21.350
you can see that there's
a significant component

00:24:21.350 --> 00:24:23.380
they are not following.

00:24:23.380 --> 00:24:26.493
One big component is a daytime nap.

00:24:27.743 --> 00:24:30.300
I often say there's a
good way to take a nap

00:24:30.300 --> 00:24:32.740
and a not-so-good way to take a nap.

00:24:32.740 --> 00:24:36.225
A good nap is one that is
more in the middle of the day,

00:24:36.225 --> 00:24:39.310
where there's a natural dip
in the middle of the day.

00:24:39.310 --> 00:24:42.860
A good nap is one that
is generally no more

00:24:42.860 --> 00:24:46.940
than an hour long and
it's maybe a half-hour

00:24:46.940 --> 00:24:49.260
to an hour would be sufficient.

00:24:49.260 --> 00:24:52.300
But what some people do who
have significant problems is

00:24:52.300 --> 00:24:53.640
they are following these things,

00:24:53.640 --> 00:24:55.337
but then they don't get a good night

00:24:55.337 --> 00:24:58.067
and then they more or less
crash, as they put it.

00:24:58.067 --> 00:25:01.790
Then they will sleep for
four hours one afternoon

00:25:01.790 --> 00:25:03.920
just to make it, but
that throws things off.

00:25:03.920 --> 00:25:07.140
So the thing again, go
back to a regular rhythm

00:25:07.140 --> 00:25:09.930
and follow the guidelines for a nap.

00:25:09.930 --> 00:25:14.030
Don't go for days without adequate sleep

00:25:14.030 --> 00:25:16.330
and then sleep for four or five hours

00:25:16.330 --> 00:25:18.713
during the day on some day.

00:25:20.550 --> 00:25:21.670
- That's really helpful.

00:25:21.670 --> 00:25:22.790
- Consistency is important.

00:25:22.790 --> 00:25:24.290
- Yeah and it's the way
you're sharing the story

00:25:24.290 --> 00:25:27.500
that seems like a lot of
times people are just using

00:25:27.500 --> 00:25:30.500
their own best educated guess
to try and address things,

00:25:30.500 --> 00:25:32.450
but that might not necessarily be

00:25:32.450 --> 00:25:34.170
what's best for the situation.

00:25:34.170 --> 00:25:36.370
- Exactly, exactly.

00:25:36.370 --> 00:25:37.410
- So that's helpful to hear.

00:25:37.410 --> 00:25:38.580
I'm sure people are,

00:25:38.580 --> 00:25:40.880
I imagine people are taking
notes or even thinking

00:25:40.880 --> 00:25:43.350
about ways to apply these pieces, as well.

00:25:43.350 --> 00:25:45.010
I was also just gonna ask about insomnia,

00:25:45.010 --> 00:25:46.810
just because anecdotally,
just talking to people,

00:25:46.810 --> 00:25:50.080
there are so many people that
say they have problems falling

00:25:50.080 --> 00:25:52.510
asleep at certain times of their lives.

00:25:52.510 --> 00:25:54.880
Are those rates changing over time

00:25:54.880 --> 00:25:59.550
or are people just more aware
of those sleep disturbances?

00:25:59.550 --> 00:26:01.640
- Yeah, I would say people are more aware

00:26:01.640 --> 00:26:05.810
because the studies showed
that about 10% of people have

00:26:05.810 --> 00:26:08.450
significant insomnia and
this is longstanding.

00:26:08.450 --> 00:26:10.470
And 10% is a big number.

00:26:10.470 --> 00:26:12.300
That has been more or less terrible.

00:26:12.300 --> 00:26:15.480
And then when we talk about insomnia,

00:26:15.480 --> 00:26:18.510
many would tend to focus on
the difficulty falling asleep

00:26:18.510 --> 00:26:21.340
in the first place, but by definition,

00:26:21.340 --> 00:26:22.910
that's not all there is.

00:26:22.910 --> 00:26:25.920
Some may fall asleep easily,

00:26:25.920 --> 00:26:28.120
but have difficulty maintaining sleep.

00:26:28.120 --> 00:26:32.440
So difficulty maintaining
sleep is also a problem.

00:26:32.440 --> 00:26:35.960
Or there are those that wake up too early

00:26:35.960 --> 00:26:37.703
and that is insomnia, too.

00:26:38.690 --> 00:26:40.730
It's important in the definition, though,

00:26:40.730 --> 00:26:44.360
to add the third component.

00:26:44.360 --> 00:26:47.040
It's not just difficulty
falling off to sleep

00:26:47.040 --> 00:26:49.640
or maintaining sleep, but it's associated

00:26:49.640 --> 00:26:53.400
with some impairment
in daytime functioning.

00:26:53.400 --> 00:26:55.493
That is important because it's,

00:26:57.132 --> 00:26:58.880
during my training in sleep medicine,

00:26:58.880 --> 00:27:01.780
this lady came in and
she was quite distressed

00:27:01.780 --> 00:27:03.243
because she had insomnia.

00:27:04.520 --> 00:27:05.630
So then we asked the question,

00:27:05.630 --> 00:27:07.880
so do you have difficulty
falling off to sleep?

00:27:07.880 --> 00:27:09.350
She says, no.

00:27:09.350 --> 00:27:11.680
Do you have difficulty staying asleep?

00:27:11.680 --> 00:27:13.060
She says, no.

00:27:13.060 --> 00:27:15.420
When you wake up in the
morning, do you feel refreshed?

00:27:15.420 --> 00:27:16.503
She says, yes,

00:27:17.360 --> 00:27:20.540
but I'm getting about six
and a half hours of sleep.

00:27:20.540 --> 00:27:22.740
Six to seven, I think.

00:27:22.740 --> 00:27:27.370
But everybody in my family
sleeps about eight to nine hours.

00:27:27.370 --> 00:27:30.580
So I have insomnia because
I have fewer hours of sleep.

00:27:30.580 --> 00:27:32.530
But the hours is not everything.

00:27:32.530 --> 00:27:36.930
So insomnia means you have
difficulty falling asleep

00:27:36.930 --> 00:27:39.320
or maintaining sleep and you also have

00:27:39.320 --> 00:27:42.960
some daytime consequence to it.

00:27:42.960 --> 00:27:45.953
So if you are sleeping fewer hours

00:27:45.953 --> 00:27:49.720
than the rest of the family,
but you feel perfectly fine,

00:27:49.720 --> 00:27:52.100
you feel perfectly refreshed,
then by definition,

00:27:52.100 --> 00:27:53.413
you do not have insomnia.

00:27:53.413 --> 00:27:54.246
- Oh, okay.

00:27:54.246 --> 00:27:55.079
- So I think it's important.

00:27:55.079 --> 00:27:57.813
It's not the number, it's
the things that go with it.

00:27:58.690 --> 00:28:01.930
And I think maybe I deviated
from what your question was,

00:28:01.930 --> 00:28:02.763
but.

00:28:02.763 --> 00:28:03.596
- Yeah, that's helpful, as well,

00:28:03.596 --> 00:28:05.310
because I know just talking with people,

00:28:05.310 --> 00:28:06.980
people from different families
that said, oh, you know,

00:28:06.980 --> 00:28:09.600
well, some people in my family
sleep four or five hours

00:28:09.600 --> 00:28:12.040
and they went in for all
the sleep studies to see

00:28:12.040 --> 00:28:12.873
is there something?

00:28:12.873 --> 00:28:14.320
But they said they've
been functioning fine.

00:28:14.320 --> 00:28:16.050
And so eventually they were
just told that that was

00:28:16.050 --> 00:28:17.590
how they operate.

00:28:17.590 --> 00:28:19.310
But then, you know, they
interact with others

00:28:19.310 --> 00:28:20.610
and people think, oh,
what's wrong with you?

00:28:20.610 --> 00:28:22.440
Why are you only sleeping
four hours a night?

00:28:22.440 --> 00:28:23.700
That can't be healthy.

00:28:23.700 --> 00:28:25.540
So what you shared is definitely helpful

00:28:25.540 --> 00:28:27.630
for that perspective, as well.

00:28:27.630 --> 00:28:28.463
- Yes.

00:28:28.463 --> 00:28:31.810
But it's also important
to add that there are some

00:28:31.810 --> 00:28:35.930
who claim that all they need
is four or five hours of sleep.

00:28:35.930 --> 00:28:40.848
The thing is that, we said the
general rule is most people,

00:28:40.848 --> 00:28:42.259
the academy stands by this,

00:28:42.259 --> 00:28:45.363
seven to nine hours of
sleep is adequate sleep.

00:28:46.748 --> 00:28:49.190
Other studies show that
when you're more or less

00:28:49.190 --> 00:28:52.430
consistently getting less
than six hours of sleep,

00:28:52.430 --> 00:28:54.760
there can be major consequences.

00:28:54.760 --> 00:28:58.880
And this includes consequences
by daytime sleepiness

00:28:58.880 --> 00:28:59.850
or tiredness.

00:28:59.850 --> 00:29:01.560
There can be cognitive impairment.

00:29:01.560 --> 00:29:04.780
You are not as sharp as you should be.

00:29:04.780 --> 00:29:07.457
And sometimes people do not recognize it.

00:29:07.457 --> 00:29:11.870
And sometimes I'll say if
somebody is an A student

00:29:11.870 --> 00:29:16.630
or A-plus student and is
performing at a B-plus level,

00:29:16.630 --> 00:29:20.440
I mean, it's not, people
will say that's acceptable.

00:29:20.440 --> 00:29:22.630
But if you are an A-plus
person functioning

00:29:22.630 --> 00:29:24.450
at B-plus level,

00:29:24.450 --> 00:29:26.340
you're actually not
functioning at your peak

00:29:26.340 --> 00:29:27.803
and you don't recognize it.

00:29:28.750 --> 00:29:33.750
So things like generally less
than six hours of sleep can be

00:29:33.750 --> 00:29:34.720
an impairment.

00:29:34.720 --> 00:29:38.703
Your risk of cardiovascular
consequences is higher.

00:29:40.240 --> 00:29:42.970
Your immunity level is high,
studies have been done.

00:29:42.970 --> 00:29:45.490
There's one that struck
me, came out recently.

00:29:45.490 --> 00:29:46.720
Just a common cold

00:29:48.500 --> 00:29:53.360
and people who were sleeping
less than six hours of sleep,

00:29:53.360 --> 00:29:54.720
didn't ask them to change anything,

00:29:54.720 --> 00:29:57.420
just selected groups and
those who are getting

00:29:57.420 --> 00:29:59.333
adequate sleep, seven to nine hours.

00:30:00.170 --> 00:30:03.490
Those sleeping less than six
hours of sleep generally,

00:30:03.490 --> 00:30:05.010
and they are healthy.

00:30:05.010 --> 00:30:10.010
When the two groups are exposed
to the common cold virus,

00:30:10.030 --> 00:30:14.420
those who sleep less than
six hours have a fourfold

00:30:14.420 --> 00:30:16.740
increased risk of getting the cold,

00:30:16.740 --> 00:30:19.103
as opposed to those who sleep adequately.

00:30:19.940 --> 00:30:22.440
I see that as important
these days of COVID,

00:30:22.440 --> 00:30:24.140
nobody has studied that.

00:30:24.140 --> 00:30:26.610
But if you are sleeping consistently less,

00:30:26.610 --> 00:30:29.100
are you a greater risk of getting COVID?

00:30:29.100 --> 00:30:31.700
We don't know that, but
we know that people have

00:30:31.700 --> 00:30:33.560
a high risk, like I said, of heart attack,

00:30:33.560 --> 00:30:35.750
they have a high risk of developing

00:30:35.750 --> 00:30:38.480
other medical consequences.

00:30:38.480 --> 00:30:40.830
So we should take insomnia seriously,

00:30:40.830 --> 00:30:43.033
it does have consequences.

00:30:44.410 --> 00:30:45.980
We are talking about mental health-wise,

00:30:45.980 --> 00:30:48.250
so my background is mental health, too.

00:30:48.250 --> 00:30:50.590
We know that those with longstanding,

00:30:50.590 --> 00:30:52.613
long-term insomnia have a greater risk

00:30:52.613 --> 00:30:56.040
of developing depression
or having anxiety problems.

00:30:56.040 --> 00:30:58.820
Those with depression are
more likely to have insomnia,

00:30:58.820 --> 00:31:01.520
but those with insomnia also more likely

00:31:01.520 --> 00:31:03.350
to develop depression.

00:31:03.350 --> 00:31:07.020
And sometimes people feel like
insomnia is just my problem,

00:31:07.020 --> 00:31:09.750
or I can just deal with it on my own,

00:31:09.750 --> 00:31:13.190
but there are times that it
does need to be looked at

00:31:13.190 --> 00:31:14.733
professionally and managed.

00:31:16.700 --> 00:31:18.860
Long answers to short questions.

00:31:18.860 --> 00:31:19.850
- All very good and very helpful.

00:31:19.850 --> 00:31:21.660
And I'm sure, again, I'm
sure that this is something

00:31:21.660 --> 00:31:22.520
that's very,

00:31:22.520 --> 00:31:24.850
that people are just thinking
about because I'm sure

00:31:24.850 --> 00:31:26.800
in people's own lives or in
people they interact with,

00:31:26.800 --> 00:31:29.010
there are lots of things
they may have noticed

00:31:29.010 --> 00:31:31.110
that they've never
really heard talked about

00:31:31.110 --> 00:31:32.860
as fully before.

00:31:32.860 --> 00:31:34.610
So it's really helpful
to have that, as well.

00:31:34.610 --> 00:31:35.770
And it makes me think about what you said

00:31:35.770 --> 00:31:39.830
about the public conversations
and the emphasis on that

00:31:39.830 --> 00:31:40.960
in the next few years,

00:31:40.960 --> 00:31:43.663
I think that's very important, as well.

00:31:45.053 --> 00:31:46.120
And I was thinking about
what you were saying

00:31:46.120 --> 00:31:48.180
about people operating at lower level

00:31:48.180 --> 00:31:51.733
that might not be their peak performance.

00:31:52.671 --> 00:31:53.930
How do you help people realize that?

00:31:53.930 --> 00:31:55.720
Because if someone has been in that,

00:31:55.720 --> 00:31:58.820
when someone's been operating
at a B plus for 10 years

00:31:58.820 --> 00:32:01.740
and that's the norm, do
you leave it that way?

00:32:01.740 --> 00:32:04.538
Do you help them try and adjust

00:32:04.538 --> 00:32:06.630
to improve their quality of life?

00:32:06.630 --> 00:32:09.940
Or how does that conversation get started?

00:32:09.940 --> 00:32:13.940
- Yeah, it's difficult to convince people

00:32:13.940 --> 00:32:16.900
when they don't see the
evidence on their own.

00:32:16.900 --> 00:32:21.610
So we talk about insomnia,
I'll shift to something else.

00:32:21.610 --> 00:32:23.730
The lazy farmer had sleep apnea.

00:32:23.730 --> 00:32:28.040
So I'll talk a little bit about
sleep apnea at this point.

00:32:28.040 --> 00:32:31.210
Sleep apnea is when you see
some of the most dramatic

00:32:31.210 --> 00:32:33.810
changes and addresses this
question about functioning

00:32:33.810 --> 00:32:37.433
at a B-plus level,
instead of A-plus level.

00:32:38.930 --> 00:32:43.670
You see somebody who comes
in, they have loud snoring.

00:32:43.670 --> 00:32:46.543
They have some tiredness and
sleepiness during the day.

00:32:47.590 --> 00:32:49.620
Often this is coming out in men.

00:32:49.620 --> 00:32:53.090
So the man comes in, not because
he thinks he has a problem,

00:32:53.090 --> 00:32:55.520
but because he's tired
of his wife complaining

00:32:55.520 --> 00:32:58.190
that he's snoring so loudly

00:32:58.190 --> 00:33:00.373
she is not getting good enough sleep.

00:33:01.510 --> 00:33:03.230
He comes in, he has a sleep study.

00:33:03.230 --> 00:33:05.720
A diagnosis of sleep apnea is made.

00:33:05.720 --> 00:33:07.103
He gets active treatment for that.

00:33:07.103 --> 00:33:11.220
One of the most common treatments
is the C-PAP continuous

00:33:11.220 --> 00:33:14.470
positive pressure device that he wears.

00:33:14.470 --> 00:33:19.470
And it's maybe surprising
or amazing, or maybe not,

00:33:19.870 --> 00:33:22.060
but people will come back and they say,

00:33:22.060 --> 00:33:24.600
I should have done this 10 years ago

00:33:24.600 --> 00:33:27.790
because all this time, so
I thought it's just my age

00:33:27.790 --> 00:33:31.050
that because now I'm 45 or 50

00:33:31.050 --> 00:33:34.200
that's why I've not been as sharp.

00:33:34.200 --> 00:33:36.980
But you treated sleep
apnea and this goes away.

00:33:36.980 --> 00:33:39.880
So that's when they recognize
that it's extremely difficult

00:33:39.880 --> 00:33:42.663
to convince people they are
functioning at a lower level

00:33:42.663 --> 00:33:44.690
'til they see it themselves.

00:33:44.690 --> 00:33:47.850
So the goal is not so
much to convince them

00:33:47.850 --> 00:33:51.680
that they will function at
a higher level, but to say,

00:33:51.680 --> 00:33:52.930
this we know is a problem.

00:33:52.930 --> 00:33:54.420
This is a medical condition.

00:33:54.420 --> 00:33:57.290
Or if it's insomnia, if it's sleep apnea.

00:33:57.290 --> 00:33:59.350
So let's treat it and see what happens.

00:33:59.350 --> 00:34:02.900
And I often offer people,
let's give it a try,

00:34:02.900 --> 00:34:04.710
let's see what happens.

00:34:04.710 --> 00:34:07.040
Another thought comes to mind.

00:34:07.040 --> 00:34:08.880
I say, I love to be proven wrong

00:34:08.880 --> 00:34:10.440
in terms of (speaker drowned out).

00:34:10.440 --> 00:34:11.460
So a lady comes in,

00:34:11.460 --> 00:34:15.670
she's averaging about
20 cups of coffee a day.

00:34:15.670 --> 00:34:19.810
And she says she has
insomnia and she needs help.

00:34:19.810 --> 00:34:22.440
And I said, well, you talk
about her sleep hygiene.

00:34:22.440 --> 00:34:24.530
One of the first things
you can do is to try

00:34:24.530 --> 00:34:27.140
and cut back down on how much coffee

00:34:27.140 --> 00:34:28.393
you are drinking per day.

00:34:29.360 --> 00:34:32.350
And she wasn't convinced it
would make any difference.

00:34:32.350 --> 00:34:34.310
But these were own
words when she came back

00:34:34.310 --> 00:34:35.910
for her first follow up.

00:34:35.910 --> 00:34:39.440
She says, I decided to do
this to prove you wrong.

00:34:39.440 --> 00:34:41.530
I was sure it wasn't going
to make any difference,

00:34:41.530 --> 00:34:43.690
but I thought, okay, I'll just cut back,

00:34:43.690 --> 00:34:46.070
I cut back to half my consumption

00:34:46.070 --> 00:34:47.310
and I'll go back to this doctor

00:34:47.310 --> 00:34:49.920
and I'll show you how wrong it is.

00:34:49.920 --> 00:34:51.580
She comes back and she's surprised.

00:34:51.580 --> 00:34:53.597
She's surprised (speaker
drowned out by laughing).

00:34:55.260 --> 00:34:56.850
So this is why I said, no,

00:34:56.850 --> 00:35:01.100
it's great to be proven wrong
on this, okay, (indistinct).

00:35:02.490 --> 00:35:05.863
So then there is, going
back to sleep apnea.

00:35:05.863 --> 00:35:09.440
I see a 70-year-old guy, he's about 70

00:35:09.440 --> 00:35:13.430
and sleep apnea can
present in unusual ways.

00:35:13.430 --> 00:35:15.730
For some men of (microphone cuts out),

00:35:15.730 --> 00:35:18.340
it can cause you to go
to the bathroom a lot,

00:35:18.340 --> 00:35:21.360
four, five, six times at night.

00:35:21.360 --> 00:35:24.310
And this was this patient's major problem.

00:35:24.310 --> 00:35:28.020
So he goes to see a urologist
and the urologist says,

00:35:28.020 --> 00:35:29.350
no, your prostate is fine.

00:35:29.350 --> 00:35:32.650
In spite of your age, we don't
need to do anything there.

00:35:32.650 --> 00:35:35.560
He comes to the sleep
clinic and he's upset.

00:35:35.560 --> 00:35:38.190
And he says, my doctor is a good doctor,

00:35:38.190 --> 00:35:41.020
which is why I followed
through, but I think he's wrong.

00:35:41.020 --> 00:35:43.850
What does waking up a lot to
go to the bathroom at night

00:35:43.850 --> 00:35:45.053
have to do with sleep?

00:35:46.550 --> 00:35:48.710
It's my waking up that
is disturbing my sleep.

00:35:48.710 --> 00:35:50.480
He should fix that, then I'll be fine.

00:35:50.480 --> 00:35:53.330
But he says I should be at
the sleep clinic, so I'm here.

00:35:54.270 --> 00:35:57.350
Or in the way you put
it, I know it's my age

00:35:57.350 --> 00:36:00.150
and it's my prostate.

00:36:00.150 --> 00:36:01.730
I can't do anything about my age.

00:36:01.730 --> 00:36:03.950
If you fix the prostate, I'll be fine.

00:36:03.950 --> 00:36:05.900
Anyway, he goes through the sleep study,

00:36:05.900 --> 00:36:07.700
he has confirmed sleep apnea.

00:36:07.700 --> 00:36:09.450
He gets treated.

00:36:09.450 --> 00:36:12.040
He comes back for his first follow up,

00:36:12.040 --> 00:36:13.870
that's about six weeks later.

00:36:13.870 --> 00:36:16.520
And he's saying, strange
thing that happened.

00:36:16.520 --> 00:36:18.110
He forgot some of our discussion.

00:36:18.110 --> 00:36:21.300
Now I wake up only once
to go to the bathroom.

00:36:21.300 --> 00:36:22.760
So then I asked him,

00:36:22.760 --> 00:36:26.220
did the urologist decide
to do your prostate?

00:36:26.220 --> 00:36:27.810
He says, no.

00:36:27.810 --> 00:36:30.420
And then I asked him,
well maybe (indistinct).

00:36:30.420 --> 00:36:32.340
So I said, are you still 70 years old?

00:36:32.340 --> 00:36:35.950
And he looked at me like,
what is he talking about?

00:36:35.950 --> 00:36:39.100
I said, you told me it was
your age and your prostate.

00:36:39.100 --> 00:36:41.828
Your prostate is still
there and you are still 70.

00:36:41.828 --> 00:36:43.995
(laughs)

00:36:45.770 --> 00:36:48.270
So it was a long way
to answer this question

00:36:48.270 --> 00:36:52.010
that you cannot really convince people

00:36:52.010 --> 00:36:53.560
until after they've had the treatment.

00:36:53.560 --> 00:36:57.040
So the goal is to try
somehow to get them to agree

00:36:57.040 --> 00:36:57.873
to the treatment.

00:36:57.873 --> 00:37:01.220
And often we'll phrase
it that this is a test.

00:37:01.220 --> 00:37:02.053
Let's do it.

00:37:02.053 --> 00:37:02.890
You don't lose anything.

00:37:02.890 --> 00:37:05.650
About the worst is you lose
a co-pay for the workup

00:37:05.650 --> 00:37:06.620
and the test.

00:37:06.620 --> 00:37:08.630
And if you prove me wrong, that's fine.

00:37:08.630 --> 00:37:09.633
But yeah, so.

00:37:11.041 --> 00:37:13.690
- Well, it's interesting because
it sounds like you're using

00:37:13.690 --> 00:37:17.190
some of the psychological
tools, as well, to get people

00:37:17.190 --> 00:37:20.340
to go through it and experience it

00:37:20.340 --> 00:37:22.850
and then see the outcome.

00:37:22.850 --> 00:37:24.590
But it sounds like it's so,

00:37:24.590 --> 00:37:27.020
I mean, it's so transformative
in a lot of ways.

00:37:27.020 --> 00:37:28.270
But I guess the other question is,

00:37:28.270 --> 00:37:30.040
'cause as you were
talking, it seems that also

00:37:30.040 --> 00:37:33.090
within the medical community,
people have to be aware.

00:37:33.090 --> 00:37:36.260
So the fact that the urologist
actually sent the patient

00:37:36.260 --> 00:37:38.027
to the sleep clinic was also important.

00:37:38.027 --> 00:37:41.120
Do you think that's
something that's uniform

00:37:41.120 --> 00:37:43.690
across the board or is there,

00:37:43.690 --> 00:37:46.620
are there disparities that
come up in terms of the access

00:37:46.620 --> 00:37:47.510
to care that people have

00:37:47.510 --> 00:37:48.880
and whether people are cognizant of that?

00:37:48.880 --> 00:37:50.760
And I mentioned that just
because of some of the things

00:37:50.760 --> 00:37:52.700
that we talk about in terms of, you know,

00:37:52.700 --> 00:37:54.860
some of this systems in place in the US

00:37:54.860 --> 00:37:56.780
and how that impacts different communities

00:37:56.780 --> 00:37:58.670
and communities of color, in particular,

00:37:58.670 --> 00:38:00.740
and whether there's equal
access to these services,

00:38:00.740 --> 00:38:02.410
especially around sleep.

00:38:02.410 --> 00:38:03.243
Because as you mentioned,

00:38:03.243 --> 00:38:06.630
it has so many impacts on so
many aspects of our health,

00:38:06.630 --> 00:38:08.980
just our physical health
and our mental health.

00:38:10.619 --> 00:38:12.010
- Yeah, a couple of things.

00:38:12.010 --> 00:38:14.040
In terms of referrals,

00:38:14.040 --> 00:38:18.600
unfortunately there are
still healthcare providers

00:38:18.600 --> 00:38:23.020
who are not paying enough
attention to sleep issues.

00:38:23.020 --> 00:38:24.770
I mean the clinics that
I've done in the past

00:38:24.770 --> 00:38:27.240
and even in my current position,

00:38:27.240 --> 00:38:31.720
you often will see the same kinds of names

00:38:31.720 --> 00:38:36.100
or certain primary physicians have

00:38:36.100 --> 00:38:39.910
a lot of referrals coming through
and some have almost none.

00:38:39.910 --> 00:38:43.150
So obviously people with
sleep problems are not

00:38:43.150 --> 00:38:45.350
pre-selecting who they would see.

00:38:45.350 --> 00:38:47.650
So that would mean that
there are some clinicians

00:38:47.650 --> 00:38:51.950
who are not paying enough
attention to send the patients.

00:38:51.950 --> 00:38:53.570
Access is a huge problem.

00:38:53.570 --> 00:38:55.950
Actually, I was looking
at something this week,

00:38:55.950 --> 00:39:00.950
the morbidity and mortality
weekly report from the CDC.

00:39:03.160 --> 00:39:08.160
The one from September 24th,
so this, yeah, this past week.

00:39:09.150 --> 00:39:12.830
It was looking at sleep in children

00:39:12.830 --> 00:39:14.750
four months old to 17 years.

00:39:14.750 --> 00:39:17.013
So children and adolescents,

00:39:17.920 --> 00:39:21.393
and it said about a third in that group,

00:39:22.290 --> 00:39:26.640
a third are not getting the
adequate duration of sleep

00:39:26.640 --> 00:39:29.920
that they need and there
are regional differences.

00:39:29.920 --> 00:39:34.653
It's about 25% in Maine,
about 49% in Mississippi.

00:39:35.510 --> 00:39:36.810
And when you look at this, and say,

00:39:36.810 --> 00:39:41.300
we do know that inadequate
sleep has health consequences,

00:39:41.300 --> 00:39:46.250
but you see greater risk
or higher prevalence

00:39:46.250 --> 00:39:50.730
in racial and ethnic minorities.

00:39:50.730 --> 00:39:54.233
And then in areas where the socioeconomic,

00:39:55.070 --> 00:39:57.070
people are at the bottom or the lower end

00:39:57.070 --> 00:39:58.950
of the socioeconomic climate.

00:39:58.950 --> 00:40:03.160
So these groups have higher
prevalence rates of almost all

00:40:03.160 --> 00:40:07.803
sleep disturbances and they
have lower rates of access.

00:40:09.550 --> 00:40:12.560
There's another thing that
makes that even more distressing

00:40:12.560 --> 00:40:13.890
is this,

00:40:13.890 --> 00:40:17.590
when it comes to the
things like sleep apnea,

00:40:17.590 --> 00:40:19.150
if you've had the same level,

00:40:19.150 --> 00:40:24.040
the consequences are often
worse, racial minorities.

00:40:24.040 --> 00:40:26.190
So there are ethnic
and racial differences.

00:40:26.190 --> 00:40:28.010
So if you have, for example,

00:40:28.010 --> 00:40:32.230
a Caucasian with a certain
degree of sleep apnea,

00:40:32.230 --> 00:40:36.930
and there's a black person
who has the same degree

00:40:36.930 --> 00:40:39.650
of sleep apnea, the chances
that the black person,

00:40:39.650 --> 00:40:43.180
even with the same access will
get more health consequences

00:40:43.180 --> 00:40:44.270
are higher.

00:40:44.270 --> 00:40:46.050
So yeah, so it's not equal.

00:40:46.050 --> 00:40:48.540
And the Academy of Sleep
Medicine right now is trying

00:40:48.540 --> 00:40:51.890
to pay attention to that,
to the racial, ethnic,

00:40:51.890 --> 00:40:55.020
and socioeconomic disparity
in the distribution

00:40:55.020 --> 00:40:58.830
and the availability
of access to treatment

00:40:58.830 --> 00:40:59.950
for these groups.

00:40:59.950 --> 00:41:01.630
- Yeah, that seems very important,

00:41:01.630 --> 00:41:03.280
especially because of the
way that you phrased for all.

00:41:03.280 --> 00:41:07.430
'Cause that seems like it will
affect all aspects of health

00:41:07.430 --> 00:41:09.240
for the individual's life.

00:41:09.240 --> 00:41:11.730
And so that just in
addition to everything else

00:41:11.730 --> 00:41:13.080
that has been discussed,

00:41:13.080 --> 00:41:14.190
obviously around health disparities,

00:41:14.190 --> 00:41:16.340
it seems like sleep is
a huge part of that,

00:41:16.340 --> 00:41:18.137
which I don't hear talked about as much.

00:41:18.137 --> 00:41:19.990
And maybe that's just
the circles that I'm in,

00:41:19.990 --> 00:41:21.630
but even in public discourse,

00:41:21.630 --> 00:41:23.900
it doesn't seem that that's
often brought to the forefront,

00:41:23.900 --> 00:41:26.630
but it sounds like hopefully
there are pushes to make sure

00:41:26.630 --> 00:41:28.860
that becomes a better
part of understanding.

00:41:28.860 --> 00:41:29.810
- Yes, like I said,

00:41:29.810 --> 00:41:31.910
the Academy of Sleep
Medicine is working on it.

00:41:31.910 --> 00:41:35.960
Yeah, but within the
general medical field,

00:41:35.960 --> 00:41:39.770
maybe we are not getting
enough attention on it.

00:41:39.770 --> 00:41:42.760
It's more recently, the
Academy's pushing the whole area,

00:41:42.760 --> 00:41:44.800
the disparity in the distribution.

00:41:44.800 --> 00:41:45.633
So

00:41:46.990 --> 00:41:47.823
first of all,

00:41:47.823 --> 00:41:52.250
in some of the groups, the
lower socioeconomic groups,

00:41:52.250 --> 00:41:55.840
people may not recognize that
there is a problem with sleep

00:41:55.840 --> 00:42:00.770
and maybe physicians or care
providers in those places,

00:42:00.770 --> 00:42:04.203
I know recognize it enough to
encourage the people to go.

00:42:05.300 --> 00:42:08.610
But then part of, increase
the level of awareness

00:42:08.610 --> 00:42:12.750
in primary care physicians
so they will recognize this

00:42:12.750 --> 00:42:16.310
and treat it because some
people are very far away

00:42:16.310 --> 00:42:19.970
from any center, any established
sleep disorder centers.

00:42:19.970 --> 00:42:22.340
But the thing is to get
primary care physicians

00:42:22.340 --> 00:42:24.560
to recognize the importance
and to address it

00:42:24.560 --> 00:42:26.313
because there are many, many things

00:42:26.313 --> 00:42:29.313
that primary care
providers can do to help.

00:42:30.350 --> 00:42:32.880
- I'm curious if you ever
see any resistance to that,

00:42:32.880 --> 00:42:33.993
because you talked about
some of the patients

00:42:33.993 --> 00:42:36.770
that are resistant to it and
don't realize there's an issue

00:42:36.770 --> 00:42:40.790
until they've tried to prove
you wrong and they've seen it.

00:42:40.790 --> 00:42:43.820
Does that ever happen with
the primary physicians

00:42:43.820 --> 00:42:46.830
in certain communities
or even in the community?

00:42:46.830 --> 00:42:48.220
Because I'm thinking also about stigma,

00:42:48.220 --> 00:42:50.540
whether there's stigmas
that are still coming

00:42:50.540 --> 00:42:52.150
into play, as well.

00:42:52.150 --> 00:42:54.070
I know I asked several
questions at once, but. (laughs)

00:42:54.070 --> 00:42:57.280
- Yeah, no, but it's
probably more difficult to,

00:42:57.280 --> 00:43:00.083
with the primary care
physicians because it's,

00:43:01.030 --> 00:43:03.210
for as long as we've done
this in the community

00:43:03.210 --> 00:43:05.200
in which I am now, there are still

00:43:05.200 --> 00:43:06.410
some primary care physicians

00:43:06.410 --> 00:43:08.720
who almost never refer a patient.

00:43:08.720 --> 00:43:11.570
So, and the knowledge is there,

00:43:11.570 --> 00:43:14.440
you do educational programs
and they attend these things,

00:43:14.440 --> 00:43:16.603
but maybe like everything else,

00:43:18.470 --> 00:43:19.990
because obviously a physician,

00:43:19.990 --> 00:43:23.960
even when they've had patients
who have been treated,

00:43:23.960 --> 00:43:25.150
it looked like for some reason,

00:43:25.150 --> 00:43:27.397
some are still not convinced

00:43:27.397 --> 00:43:29.920
and they still wouldn't send patient.

00:43:29.920 --> 00:43:34.170
So in a sense, it's easier
to convert, so to speak,

00:43:34.170 --> 00:43:36.560
the patient who has had
a positive experience

00:43:36.560 --> 00:43:39.887
then physician who does
not recognize this.

00:43:39.887 --> 00:43:42.200
- Yeah, that's a really good point.

00:43:42.200 --> 00:43:43.550
So people who are able to just share that

00:43:43.550 --> 00:43:45.050
in their communities, as well.

00:43:45.990 --> 00:43:48.770
Have you seen that happen in
some ways or in some places,

00:43:48.770 --> 00:43:50.283
either here or in Ghana?

00:43:52.040 --> 00:43:54.510
Have you seen those who've actually gone

00:43:54.510 --> 00:43:57.000
through the experience as patients,

00:43:57.000 --> 00:43:58.447
have you seen them have
impact in the communities,

00:43:58.447 --> 00:44:00.460
either in the states or in Ghana?

00:44:00.460 --> 00:44:01.613
- Yes, they have actually.

00:44:01.613 --> 00:44:05.417
When I started here in Grand Rapids,

00:44:05.417 --> 00:44:07.890
that one of my colleagues used to say,

00:44:07.890 --> 00:44:11.460
he's a pulmonary physician
whose friends were thinking

00:44:11.460 --> 00:44:15.530
he was leaving medicine
by going into sleep.

00:44:15.530 --> 00:44:17.190
But then he always would encourage you,

00:44:17.190 --> 00:44:20.618
said our best promoters

00:44:20.618 --> 00:44:23.210
for the sleep clinic are the patients.

00:44:23.210 --> 00:44:24.700
So you treat a patient well.

00:44:24.700 --> 00:44:27.660
And I do have a lot of patients who tell

00:44:27.660 --> 00:44:30.880
their family members and friends
and colleagues to come in

00:44:30.880 --> 00:44:33.890
and the physician sometimes
becomes secondary.

00:44:33.890 --> 00:44:35.710
They will get a good
treatment and they will tell

00:44:35.710 --> 00:44:39.990
their physician that, well,
this is what I realized I have,

00:44:39.990 --> 00:44:42.080
my cousin needs to go, too.

00:44:42.080 --> 00:44:45.150
So can you refer my cousin
to the sleep clinic?

00:44:45.150 --> 00:44:47.630
So sometimes there is the
patient who drives it.

00:44:47.630 --> 00:44:52.630
Yeah, so the patients drive
this in a lot of ways.

00:44:53.130 --> 00:44:55.830
Not so much in Ghana, and
now I've started a relation

00:44:55.830 --> 00:44:57.300
with Nigeria.

00:44:57.300 --> 00:44:59.040
I recently learned,

00:44:59.040 --> 00:45:02.218
you can leave your country
and forget the culture.

00:45:02.218 --> 00:45:06.670
In that culture, you don't talk
about your health problems.

00:45:06.670 --> 00:45:09.510
So that I've seen patients,
and actually this last week,

00:45:09.510 --> 00:45:10.710
that somebody actually told me,

00:45:10.710 --> 00:45:12.920
I'm not going to let anyone know.

00:45:12.920 --> 00:45:14.830
So they got a patient,
even when they've had

00:45:14.830 --> 00:45:16.350
a positive response,

00:45:16.350 --> 00:45:18.610
I don't got to share that
information with anyone else.

00:45:18.610 --> 00:45:23.610
So yes, patients drive the
growth of the clinic here,

00:45:25.080 --> 00:45:28.120
but in a different
culture that doesn't work.

00:45:28.120 --> 00:45:32.040
So then you have to work
through the primary physicians.

00:45:32.040 --> 00:45:35.860
Like we said again, that role
of authority in that context.

00:45:35.860 --> 00:45:38.980
So it going to be more
important in that context

00:45:38.980 --> 00:45:41.664
to convince the physicians
to send patients.

00:45:41.664 --> 00:45:43.690
Or I see the patients can refer themselves

00:45:43.690 --> 00:45:45.640
or convince their doctors to send them.

00:45:47.160 --> 00:45:48.640
- Well, it seems a lot just being aware of

00:45:48.640 --> 00:45:51.820
just the culture within, like
you said, that it's operating

00:45:51.820 --> 00:45:53.190
and the system's in place.

00:45:53.190 --> 00:45:54.970
- Yeah, yeah.

00:45:54.970 --> 00:45:55.903
Very much so.

00:45:56.952 --> 00:45:58.720
And obviously when it comes to sleep,

00:45:58.720 --> 00:46:00.520
there are various sleep disorders.

00:46:00.520 --> 00:46:03.620
I think you had a question
of when does one treat it

00:46:03.620 --> 00:46:08.323
on their own and when do you
seek help from a professional?

00:46:09.881 --> 00:46:11.950
A lot of people with insomnia will try

00:46:11.950 --> 00:46:14.560
and manage on their own, but,

00:46:14.560 --> 00:46:17.210
and there's habits and
there are behavioral things

00:46:17.210 --> 00:46:18.143
you can do to improve things.

00:46:18.143 --> 00:46:20.240
We talk about their sleep
hygiene and there are more

00:46:20.240 --> 00:46:21.610
specific things,

00:46:21.610 --> 00:46:24.620
but even we've done all of
these, the usual things,

00:46:24.620 --> 00:46:26.300
you know, and they are not working,

00:46:26.300 --> 00:46:29.540
it's important then to
seek professional help

00:46:29.540 --> 00:46:33.580
because of the consequences
of untreated insomnia

00:46:33.580 --> 00:46:36.180
or excessive sleepiness.

00:46:36.180 --> 00:46:38.170
Sleep apnea and some of
the conditions that cause

00:46:38.170 --> 00:46:42.740
excessive sleepiness can be
dangerous because people can get

00:46:42.740 --> 00:46:44.700
to the point of falling asleep driving,

00:46:44.700 --> 00:46:48.400
which puts the person at risk
and puts others on the road

00:46:48.400 --> 00:46:50.003
at risk, as well.

00:46:52.280 --> 00:46:54.420
So that's an insomnia thing,
if it's not improving.

00:46:54.420 --> 00:46:58.510
You should avoid taking sleeping
pills on a long-term basis.

00:46:58.510 --> 00:47:02.260
A sleeping pill may have a
place in managing short-term

00:47:02.260 --> 00:47:05.170
insomnia problems, but if you are taking

00:47:05.170 --> 00:47:08.140
whatever you are taking, if
you see the dose going up,

00:47:08.140 --> 00:47:09.340
the dose keeps climbing

00:47:09.340 --> 00:47:11.700
and the effectiveness is decreasing.

00:47:11.700 --> 00:47:14.653
That may be one indication to see someone.

00:47:15.950 --> 00:47:19.210
For those who have problem with snoring

00:47:20.070 --> 00:47:22.380
and begin to develop daytime sleepiness,

00:47:22.380 --> 00:47:25.170
the snoring itself may not
necessarily mean you have

00:47:25.170 --> 00:47:28.490
a sleep apnea or major sleep problem.

00:47:28.490 --> 00:47:30.820
But if there is disruption of your sleep,

00:47:30.820 --> 00:47:33.660
you don't feel as refreshed
when you wake up in the morning,

00:47:33.660 --> 00:47:37.390
or you are beginning to have
some daytime sleepiness,

00:47:37.390 --> 00:47:39.890
or in terms of the sleep apnea,

00:47:39.890 --> 00:47:44.520
if whoever is sleeping with
you or anyone who has observed

00:47:44.520 --> 00:47:46.370
you sleeping comments that it looks

00:47:46.370 --> 00:47:49.070
like your breathing gets
interrupted at night,

00:47:49.070 --> 00:47:52.213
then you want to seek
professional help for that

00:47:52.213 --> 00:47:55.160
because that's when you would
need a sleep study to clarify

00:47:55.160 --> 00:47:56.540
what is going on.

00:47:56.540 --> 00:47:58.530
Again, it's because of the consequences,

00:47:58.530 --> 00:48:02.440
risk of getting a stroke is higher,

00:48:02.440 --> 00:48:04.503
risk of getting a heart attack is higher.

00:48:05.420 --> 00:48:09.290
The cognitive impairment
can come on slowly,

00:48:09.290 --> 00:48:11.270
but it can be significant.

00:48:11.270 --> 00:48:14.580
Again, this is one of those
things that people will recover

00:48:14.580 --> 00:48:16.873
and go back to work.

00:48:18.300 --> 00:48:19.920
We relate this to faith, as well.

00:48:19.920 --> 00:48:23.540
So then the example of
a pastor comes to mind

00:48:23.540 --> 00:48:26.150
who has sleep apnea and
didn't recognize it.

00:48:26.150 --> 00:48:28.200
Things were not prominent,

00:48:28.200 --> 00:48:31.710
but it was after he got
treated that he said,

00:48:31.710 --> 00:48:34.450
so this is why I was getting
to the point I was having

00:48:34.450 --> 00:48:36.833
difficulty preparing my sermons.

00:48:36.833 --> 00:48:39.830
I mean, certainly I work
on it and then I'm standing

00:48:39.830 --> 00:48:42.560
at the pulpit and my
things were not clicking.

00:48:42.560 --> 00:48:44.540
And I thought, well,
there's a spiritual thing,

00:48:44.540 --> 00:48:45.760
I'm not praying enough and things.

00:48:45.760 --> 00:48:47.930
Well his sleep apnea was not treated,

00:48:47.930 --> 00:48:51.510
so his brain was not
functioning adequately.

00:48:51.510 --> 00:48:53.820
So when things are not
improving that you've tried

00:48:53.820 --> 00:48:56.730
on your own, then it's
time to see a professional

00:48:56.730 --> 00:48:58.220
to see what's going on.

00:48:58.220 --> 00:49:00.577
- Those are really helpful,
really helpful tips, as well.

00:49:00.577 --> 00:49:03.074
And I'm sure again, that
people, this is gonna,

00:49:03.074 --> 00:49:04.480
these are things that people
are going to be thinking.

00:49:04.480 --> 00:49:05.340
Like you said, someone who said,

00:49:05.340 --> 00:49:07.340
I wish I had known this 10 years earlier

00:49:07.340 --> 00:49:08.330
and those types of things.

00:49:08.330 --> 00:49:10.910
So, I appreciate you sharing those.

00:49:10.910 --> 00:49:11.743
I'm also curious though,

00:49:11.743 --> 00:49:14.500
because you talked about how
sleep affects overall health

00:49:14.500 --> 00:49:16.310
and then how it affects mental health.

00:49:16.310 --> 00:49:19.480
And then if people have a
higher prevalence for depression

00:49:19.480 --> 00:49:21.490
or anxiety because of sleep disturbances

00:49:21.490 --> 00:49:22.770
or the other way around.

00:49:22.770 --> 00:49:26.420
How do you advise people
what to tackle first?

00:49:26.420 --> 00:49:28.000
So, I mean, if they're
noticing that they're having

00:49:28.000 --> 00:49:29.220
some challenges with anxiety,

00:49:29.220 --> 00:49:31.100
but also having challenges with sleep.

00:49:31.100 --> 00:49:35.030
Where should people go
to try and address that?

00:49:35.030 --> 00:49:36.543
Are there parts they should
try and do their own?

00:49:36.543 --> 00:49:37.610
Do they have to go,

00:49:37.610 --> 00:49:38.680
because there's also the challenge of,

00:49:38.680 --> 00:49:41.430
well, how many different
providers do they have to go to,

00:49:41.430 --> 00:49:43.840
to deal with the intersections?

00:49:43.840 --> 00:49:44.673
- Yeah.

00:49:45.746 --> 00:49:48.500
I think it's, as a general
rule, is always good to start

00:49:48.500 --> 00:49:53.490
with your primary care provider
and whether it's anxiety

00:49:53.490 --> 00:49:56.250
or the sleep thing.

00:49:56.250 --> 00:49:59.550
Anxiety can be so disabling,
anxiety and the depression.

00:49:59.550 --> 00:50:03.420
And I say that it will
make sense to approach

00:50:03.420 --> 00:50:06.563
the anxiety or depression initially.

00:50:08.320 --> 00:50:13.290
From the standpoint of both
counselings kinds of treatment,

00:50:13.290 --> 00:50:15.593
as well as medication kinds of treatment,

00:50:16.500 --> 00:50:17.510
you would expect that.

00:50:17.510 --> 00:50:18.790
And I think most of it,

00:50:18.790 --> 00:50:22.840
the primary provider who
addresses the depression

00:50:22.840 --> 00:50:24.500
and anxiety, if they are using counselor

00:50:24.500 --> 00:50:28.350
or the psychologist, will
give input that will probably

00:50:28.350 --> 00:50:31.117
impact positively on their sleep, as well.

00:50:31.117 --> 00:50:35.190
But when it comes to
medication management, many,

00:50:35.190 --> 00:50:37.430
I was going to say most,
but that's in the past,

00:50:37.430 --> 00:50:38.450
things have changed.

00:50:38.450 --> 00:50:42.680
But still many antidepressant
medications and medications

00:50:42.680 --> 00:50:46.920
for anxiety have an effect
on promoting sleep, as well.

00:50:46.920 --> 00:50:50.120
So the single agent can
address both the depression

00:50:50.120 --> 00:50:53.090
and the sleep, so that
is a place to start.

00:50:53.090 --> 00:50:55.600
But if the depression
and anxiety are improving

00:50:55.600 --> 00:50:58.020
and the sleep is still
not improving enough,

00:50:58.020 --> 00:51:00.583
then you may consider
going to a sleep clinic.

00:51:01.430 --> 00:51:04.250
But if the major problem
is something like snoring

00:51:04.250 --> 00:51:08.410
or the sleep is so extreme
that somebody cannot function

00:51:08.410 --> 00:51:09.243
during the day.

00:51:09.243 --> 00:51:13.180
And a lot of patients will
be able to identify which is

00:51:13.180 --> 00:51:14.290
more prominent.

00:51:14.290 --> 00:51:17.960
When their sleep is more
disabling, it's more significant.

00:51:17.960 --> 00:51:20.430
It may not be critical
where you start from.

00:51:20.430 --> 00:51:24.180
Most sleep clinicians are
aware of the depression

00:51:24.180 --> 00:51:25.810
and anxiety component of things.

00:51:25.810 --> 00:51:28.500
So when you see them and they say,

00:51:28.500 --> 00:51:31.030
well, this is not so much the sleep,

00:51:31.030 --> 00:51:33.410
it's more the anxiety and depression.

00:51:33.410 --> 00:51:36.520
There's an interesting thing here.

00:51:36.520 --> 00:51:40.680
The stigma about mental
illness is decreasing,

00:51:40.680 --> 00:51:41.753
but it's still there.

00:51:43.537 --> 00:51:46.220
And there are many people who prefer to go

00:51:46.220 --> 00:51:48.700
to the sleep clinic first

00:51:48.700 --> 00:51:50.150
not because that's the most part,

00:51:50.150 --> 00:51:51.850
but that's more acceptable.

00:51:51.850 --> 00:51:54.863
A sleep problem, it's funny the way.

00:51:54.863 --> 00:51:57.670
A sleep problem is a
medical, biological problem.

00:51:57.670 --> 00:52:02.123
But as mental illness
problem is non-medical,

00:52:02.123 --> 00:52:04.597
it's your mind is doing something

00:52:04.597 --> 00:52:09.203
and that's not as
acceptable to most people.

00:52:10.750 --> 00:52:14.650
And again, in the faith
community, there has been.

00:52:14.650 --> 00:52:17.210
Again, it's proving that if you have

00:52:17.210 --> 00:52:21.600
a mental health problem,
then it's a lack of faith

00:52:21.600 --> 00:52:25.686
or it's your poor decision-making
or it's your poor choices.

00:52:25.686 --> 00:52:27.103
That's what is causing it.

00:52:28.400 --> 00:52:30.310
That may not necessarily be solved.

00:52:30.310 --> 00:52:32.243
But as I was thinking about this,

00:52:33.700 --> 00:52:36.720
it doesn't mean that it's your fault.

00:52:36.720 --> 00:52:38.470
Although you may have
something to do with it.

00:52:38.470 --> 00:52:41.590
In the same way that
somebody's eating habits

00:52:41.590 --> 00:52:45.390
and their lifestyle may promote
their risk of developing

00:52:45.390 --> 00:52:47.133
diabetes, type two diabetes.

00:52:47.980 --> 00:52:51.110
So somebody's poor choices
and poor habits may lead

00:52:51.110 --> 00:52:54.200
to a sleep problem or a
mental health problem.

00:52:54.200 --> 00:52:56.960
But on the other hand,
there can be somebody

00:52:56.960 --> 00:52:58.940
who develops type one diabetes.

00:52:58.940 --> 00:53:01.570
The childhood who develops
childhood diabetes,

00:53:01.570 --> 00:53:02.620
they have done all the right things.

00:53:02.620 --> 00:53:05.180
There are some things we inherit.

00:53:05.180 --> 00:53:07.610
So we should look at both the same way.

00:53:07.610 --> 00:53:09.220
You can develop diabetes,

00:53:09.220 --> 00:53:12.014
even when you've done
all the right things,

00:53:12.014 --> 00:53:13.100
but you can develop diabetes

00:53:13.100 --> 00:53:15.170
because you have done some wrong thing.

00:53:15.170 --> 00:53:17.570
So in the mental health arena,

00:53:17.570 --> 00:53:20.590
there's no reason we should
look at it any differently.

00:53:20.590 --> 00:53:22.640
You can develop a sleep
problem when you've done

00:53:22.640 --> 00:53:23.690
all the right things,

00:53:24.800 --> 00:53:26.730
but can also develop a sleep problem

00:53:26.730 --> 00:53:28.250
because you haven't done the right thing.

00:53:28.250 --> 00:53:30.530
So it's important to pay attention to that

00:53:30.530 --> 00:53:33.670
because I think we run the risk sometimes

00:53:33.670 --> 00:53:35.180
of excusing people,

00:53:35.180 --> 00:53:37.056
because we want to look at the biological.

00:53:37.056 --> 00:53:40.080
No, it has nothing to
do with what you did,

00:53:40.080 --> 00:53:41.420
it's all just biological.

00:53:41.420 --> 00:53:44.240
It's the holistic idea of things.

00:53:44.240 --> 00:53:46.060
And again, you asked me this,

00:53:46.060 --> 00:53:50.870
you ask it the whole thing
about where does faith come in.

00:53:50.870 --> 00:53:52.920
God made us one.

00:53:52.920 --> 00:53:55.777
We have the physical
component of ourselves,

00:53:55.777 --> 00:53:58.940
our emotional component,
our spiritual component.

00:53:58.940 --> 00:54:02.020
And we like to separate
the different parts of it.

00:54:02.020 --> 00:54:03.690
It should be all one whole thing.

00:54:03.690 --> 00:54:08.380
And in all of these areas,
it may be pre-determined

00:54:08.380 --> 00:54:09.760
or nothing we did wrong.

00:54:09.760 --> 00:54:12.550
That is all the areas,
in all of the areas,

00:54:12.550 --> 00:54:16.130
it may be something we need to do right.

00:54:16.130 --> 00:54:18.290
Because I think sometimes
we excuse people,

00:54:18.290 --> 00:54:20.210
no, you didn't do anything wrong.

00:54:20.210 --> 00:54:22.930
Well, maybe wrong is not the right term,

00:54:22.930 --> 00:54:25.460
but no, you have some responsibility.

00:54:25.460 --> 00:54:28.540
There are some things you
probably didn't do as well

00:54:28.540 --> 00:54:31.570
and some things you can
improve to change things.

00:54:31.570 --> 00:54:35.387
So I shouldn't go on, then
I'll become like I'm preaching.

00:54:35.387 --> 00:54:37.334
(laughs)

00:54:37.334 --> 00:54:38.760
- But that was so well said.

00:54:38.760 --> 00:54:40.050
I mean, just all the different components

00:54:40.050 --> 00:54:42.170
that you've brought in together,

00:54:42.170 --> 00:54:44.530
especially about the holistic
approach to these things.

00:54:44.530 --> 00:54:47.740
So I think it's not easy,
but important for us

00:54:47.740 --> 00:54:49.880
to continue to wrestle through.

00:54:49.880 --> 00:54:51.400
And of course, even as you were talking,

00:54:51.400 --> 00:54:54.310
I'm seeing where some of my
own influences in thinking

00:54:54.310 --> 00:54:56.400
came from because you could
be hosting this podcast

00:54:56.400 --> 00:54:57.913
with those themes and just all the things

00:54:57.913 --> 00:54:59.490
(microphone cuts out) incorporate.

00:54:59.490 --> 00:55:03.070
So it's a good reminder to
me, also, of the example

00:55:03.070 --> 00:55:04.570
that you've set in your profession

00:55:04.570 --> 00:55:06.210
and how that is something
that I've been able

00:55:06.210 --> 00:55:09.090
to also carry out from
you without even realizing

00:55:09.090 --> 00:55:11.620
how much of that actually
might've originated

00:55:11.620 --> 00:55:13.840
with you or those that
influenced you, as well.

00:55:13.840 --> 00:55:15.700
So, I mean, I think it's very well said

00:55:15.700 --> 00:55:17.490
and just all the different
components that we have

00:55:17.490 --> 00:55:21.750
to think about versus just
trying to put things in one box

00:55:21.750 --> 00:55:22.620
or another,

00:55:22.620 --> 00:55:24.130
because they're all interplaying.

00:55:24.130 --> 00:55:25.960
I mean, even as you were
talking that people were saying,

00:55:25.960 --> 00:55:28.530
well, this is because of
your own poor decision-making

00:55:28.530 --> 00:55:30.700
that you have this health
ailment or this sleep issue.

00:55:30.700 --> 00:55:32.620
But then you also said
that the sleep can lead

00:55:32.620 --> 00:55:34.030
to poor decision making.

00:55:34.030 --> 00:55:37.170
So it seems like those
two can't be divorced

00:55:37.170 --> 00:55:39.380
and it's really important to help people.

00:55:39.380 --> 00:55:40.750
Have you seen, as a closing question,

00:55:40.750 --> 00:55:43.960
have you seen us as a society
get better about moving

00:55:43.960 --> 00:55:47.440
towards what you just stated
for that holistic approach?

00:55:47.440 --> 00:55:49.570
Or are there, I mean,

00:55:49.570 --> 00:55:52.270
clearly there's still ways
we need to grow in that,

00:55:52.270 --> 00:55:54.610
but have you seen it
shifting or are we still far

00:55:54.610 --> 00:55:55.443
from where we need to be here?

00:55:55.443 --> 00:55:56.276
- Yes.

00:55:56.276 --> 00:55:59.080
No, I think there's a
definite shift, okay.

00:55:59.080 --> 00:56:01.470
And you are an example of that.

00:56:01.470 --> 00:56:06.470
Okay, so the one way (indistinct)
Duke when we started doing

00:56:06.970 --> 00:56:10.770
faith and health, it was on the side.

00:56:10.770 --> 00:56:13.588
It wasn't part of the program.

00:56:13.588 --> 00:56:16.363
And there was a lot of resistance to that.

00:56:16.363 --> 00:56:21.363
I mean it's 30 years ago,
I mean, 40 years ago,

00:56:21.480 --> 00:56:24.020
no university would be
hosting this. (laughs)

00:56:24.020 --> 00:56:26.360
No university would
want it's name attached

00:56:26.360 --> 00:56:27.853
to what we are doing now.

00:56:29.610 --> 00:56:33.920
So it's moved, it's moved a
lot and it's good to see that.

00:56:33.920 --> 00:56:35.550
Yeah, so.

00:56:35.550 --> 00:56:38.371
I mean, we still have ways
to go and there are still

00:56:38.371 --> 00:56:39.210
areas to develop.

00:56:39.210 --> 00:56:40.810
But no, as I look at you,

00:56:40.810 --> 00:56:42.783
I see where your influences come from.

00:56:44.640 --> 00:56:46.910
But as I'm looking at you doing this,

00:56:46.910 --> 00:56:49.910
I feel like where you are
doing so much greater work

00:56:49.910 --> 00:56:54.720
than I ever did and I don't
see myself doing a whole lot.

00:56:54.720 --> 00:56:56.610
So, we are very thankful.

00:56:56.610 --> 00:57:00.460
No, but we've made progress
and I think we'll continue

00:57:00.460 --> 00:57:01.830
to make progress.

00:57:01.830 --> 00:57:04.400
And it's more open and
it's more the forum,

00:57:04.400 --> 00:57:07.180
the things you've done with
the Town Hall meetings.

00:57:07.180 --> 00:57:10.490
Again, these were done
on a small-scale basis.

00:57:10.490 --> 00:57:15.490
So we are way ahead of where
we were 20, 30 years ago,

00:57:15.610 --> 00:57:19.420
and even 10 years ago, so
we are making progress.

00:57:19.420 --> 00:57:22.203
But we shouldn't just stop now

00:57:22.203 --> 00:57:24.190
(indistinct) we've achieved our goal.

00:57:24.190 --> 00:57:27.590
As Paul says, we keep
striving for the goal

00:57:27.590 --> 00:57:29.023
and we are not there yet.

00:57:31.318 --> 00:57:32.151
And so long as we live,

00:57:32.151 --> 00:57:35.793
we need to keep striving
to reach higher levels.

00:57:36.930 --> 00:57:39.370
- Well, I'm grateful to be
able to play a part in that,

00:57:39.370 --> 00:57:40.980
as well, and to carry the legacy on.

00:57:40.980 --> 00:57:43.910
And it's helpful just to have
that context reminder to know

00:57:43.910 --> 00:57:47.790
even just what we're doing now
and how much growth we've had

00:57:47.790 --> 00:57:49.800
as a society and as
universities to be able to have

00:57:49.800 --> 00:57:51.690
those types of intersecting conversations.

00:57:51.690 --> 00:57:53.470
And to really think about
ourselves as whole people.

00:57:53.470 --> 00:57:56.582
So I'm (speakers talking
over each other) continue.

00:57:56.582 --> 00:57:58.260
- So I hope some of the things
I've said will be helpful

00:57:58.260 --> 00:57:59.403
to some people.

00:58:01.870 --> 00:58:05.660
But if it's only one person I
said, it's, I mean in a sense,

00:58:05.660 --> 00:58:08.510
I went into mental health
for one or two people.

00:58:08.510 --> 00:58:10.600
I went to sleep medicine for one person.

00:58:10.600 --> 00:58:13.170
So yeah, if one person benefits from this,

00:58:13.170 --> 00:58:14.190
I suppose it's good (microphone cuts out).

00:58:14.190 --> 00:58:16.430
- Yep, and I know that many, many have,

00:58:16.430 --> 00:58:18.667
and I think that will continue, as well.

00:58:18.667 --> 00:58:21.510
So thank you so much for
joining the Addy Hour,

00:58:21.510 --> 00:58:24.810
your namesake podcast
in the sense. (laughs)

00:58:24.810 --> 00:58:27.220
So I'm grateful and I know
this is definitely gonna bless

00:58:27.220 --> 00:58:28.123
a lot of people,

00:58:29.140 --> 00:58:30.970
something that's been gratifying for me.

00:58:30.970 --> 00:58:32.730
And I always learn more every time I talk

00:58:32.730 --> 00:58:33.680
through these things with you,

00:58:33.680 --> 00:58:36.390
as surprising as that may seem to some.

00:58:36.390 --> 00:58:37.600
- Thank you.

00:58:37.600 --> 00:58:39.010
- Great having you on the Addy Hour

00:58:39.010 --> 00:58:40.670
and I hope that you all
enjoyed this episode

00:58:40.670 --> 00:58:42.100
and share it widely because I think

00:58:42.100 --> 00:58:42.933
there's so many important things here

00:58:42.933 --> 00:58:45.622
that people will benefit from, as well.

00:58:45.622 --> 00:58:46.455
Until next time.

